## Outcome

Author `docs/milestones/v0.92/MILESTONE_RUNBOOK.md` as a source-grounded operating and recovery runbook for Planning #1, Planning #2, and Workers #1-4. The runbook must preserve the distinction between observed fact, planning recommendation, and known limitation; it is operational guidance, not evidence that a release, ADR, merge, deployment, or lifecycle transition occurred.

## Required scope

The runbook must capture and reconcile the following milestone practices from authoritative repository, issue, PR, receipt, and operator evidence:

- operator directives and authority boundaries, including who may plan, implement, review, publish, merge, close, or recover;
- issue decomposition ceremony, parent/child ownership, coordination-only parents, exact dependency ordering, and preservation of already-bound or active work;
- issue-bound and planning-session goals, including when goals remain active and the truthful completion/blocked boundary;
- cross-lane communication among Planning #1/#2 and Workers #1-4, with changed-state reporting, collision alerts, exact-head handoffs, and no unsupported design authority transfer;
- the three-item canary pattern used before broad metadata or ceremony waves, including stop conditions and postmortem/readback expectations;
- canonical title standards for milestone/domain tags, child slice notation, coordination parents, bounded outcomes, repository identity, dependencies, and operator gates;
- the #119 fresh-session review rule: typed assignment before activity, canonical fresh-session UUID, no inherited implementation context, exact clean scope/SHA, read-only findings-first review, and a new fresh session after substantive fixes; a generic subagent review is not sufficient;
- clean-main monitoring, root/worktree cleanliness, collision detection, and fast-forward-only main synchronization after an authorized merge;
- dependency, serial merge, exact-head review, publication, required-check, optional-CI, and paid-runner gates, including explicit operator/cost authority where required;
- root-write incident response: stop the wave after the current atomic action, preserve evidence, identify provenance, report changed paths and owners, avoid deletion/rollback without authority, and recover through the correct typed owner;
- typed C-SDLC v2 ceremony only, including split GitHub owners, deterministic operation keys, exact-read reconciliation, and prohibition on raw GitHub writes for covered actions;
- current-repository versus legacy-repository issue identity, with explicit links and no implication that a legacy issue was migrated, closed, or superseded unless live evidence proves it;
- cloud-Polis deferral: #122 precedes #256 in a later sprint, while current `agent-logic/agent-design-language#345` is limited to AWS GPU Shepherd runner enablement/proof and does not own cloud-Polis deployment or birthday-demo authority;
- usage-below-5% escalation: report the condition promptly to Planning #1/operator and preserve enough session state for recovery rather than silently abandoning an owned lane;
- continuous movement expectations: advance safe independent work, keep serial gates closed until proven, suppress unchanged watch noise, and surface blockers/collisions promptly;
- lessons learned and bounded tooling follow-ons, clearly separated from already-authorized milestone work and without silently expanding implementation scope.

## Acceptance criteria

- The new file exists at exactly `docs/milestones/v0.92/MILESTONE_RUNBOOK.md` and is navigable by role, ceremony, gate, incident, and recovery scenario.
- Every factual claim is traceable to current repository artifacts, typed receipts, live issue/PR state, or an explicit operator directive; recommendations and limitations are labeled as such.
- Commands, binary owners, repository names, issue identities, branch/worktree expectations, and filesystem paths are checked against current source and are truthful.
- The runbook explains the three-item canary and stop/readback contract, #119 fresh-session review compliance, clean-main/fast-forward monitoring, dependency and paid-runner gates, root-write response, and current-versus-legacy identity without overstating authority.
- The cloud-Polis deferral is stated exactly: #122 -> #256 later; current #345 remains GPU-runner enablement only.
- The document makes no release, ADR approval, implementation, deployment, publication, merge, closeout, or lifecycle-completion claim not proven by cited evidence.
- The completed document receives a #119-compliant fresh-session, exact-head, findings-first review before publication; substantive fixes require a new fresh review session.
- Any unresolved ambiguity is recorded as a limitation or follow-on rather than converted into invented fact.

## Validation

- Check all referenced paths, commands, issue/PR identities, and typed-owner names against the current repository and live tracker state.
- Run the smallest focused documentation/path/link validation applicable to the touched file.
- Review the exact substantive head through the #119 fresh-session procedure and record findings/dispositions truthfully.

## Non-goals

- No issue-graph restructuring or updates to existing issues.
- No provider, cloud, mailbox, hosting, directory, AWS, CI-runner, or paid-runner action.
- No merge, release, ADR approval, publication, closeout, bootstrap, bind, lifecycle repair, or implementation work.
- No mutation of active worker worktrees or milestone lifecycle cards.
- No claim that documenting a process proves that the process, milestone, or release has completed.

